  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1003 PM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nWV imagery shows weakening upper ridge extending from SoCal down to \nnorthern Mexico. Meanwhile small Pacific upper low now over southern \nUtah with clouds and a few showers covering much of Colorado and \nstarting to seep south into New Mexico. Elsewhere Rachel continues \nto churn well off the southern Baja coast, while a weak upper low \nsits well off the California coast. \n\nWednesday and Thursday...persistent low level moisture has \nallowed good afternoon CU buildups today and we should see some \nmore of that on Wednesday. Strong capping between 500-700 mb will \nstunt any growth however. The upper low over Utah will continue to\ndrop south, over northern Arizona/New Mexico line Wednesday \nafternoon, then down to southern New Mexico/west Texas by Thursday\nmorning. Expect a few showers/thunderstorms to develop over the \nGila/Black Range Wed afternoon. A few showers could develop with \nthe upper low's southeast movement over the central CWA Wed night.\nSlight chance of showers continue on Thursday for the mountains \nand possibly west Texas. \n\nFriday through Sunday...upper ridge mostly vanished as the offshore \nPacific low and TS Rachel merge and move to near the northern Baja \nby Sunday. Mid level flow ahead of this merging system will increase \nout of the south, slowly bringing some moisture with it. PWs \nincrease to around one inch. Thus by Saturday afternoon much of the \narea could see isolated showers or thunderstorms. By Saturday night \nand Sunday strong mid-level flow develops, increasing moisture with \nthe help of some Rachel's moisture. Expect scattered \nshowers/thunderstorms Saturday night and again Sunday afternoon and \nevening.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...good moisture continues and both the GFS/ECMWF \nshow the remnants of Simon moving up to the area by Monday night \ninto Tuesday. Thus, expect some heavier rainfall and a risk of \nflooding Mon night/Tues as PWs approach record levels again. The \nmost current model runs show areas from Sierra and Dona Ana Counties \neast could see the most rain. Ensemble QPF percentiles from both \nGFS/ECMWF are both much lower than deterministic so confidence still \non the low side but definitely worth watching.",
